<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manuʻa Islands</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Manuʻa Islands are a group of three volcanic islands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tau-american-samoa">Taʻu</a>, Ofu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olosega">Olosega</a>, at the eastern end of the Samoan archipelago, about 100 kilometres east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutuila">Tutuila</a>. Seat of the ancient title of Tuʻi Manuʻa, they were ceded to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1904 and are part of American Samoa. Part of the National Park of American Samoa lies on Taʻu and Ofu.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manua-islands.jpg" alt="Manuʻa Islands" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eric Guinther (talk · contribs) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AmSamoa_Ofu_442.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
